#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subset of $S=\\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}: {-3} \\leq 2n+1 \\leq 7 \\}$?\n\n1. $\\{-2, 0, 4 \\}$\n2. $\\{0, 3 \\}$\n3. $\\{-3, 1, 3 \\}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $S$. Solving the given inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\le 2n + 1 & \\le 7 \\\\ -4 \\le 2n & \\le 6 \\\\ -2 \\le n & \\le 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $S$ contains all integers between $-2$ and $3$ (including $-2$ and $3$):\n\n$$\nS = \\{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}\n$$\n\nA set is a subset of $S$ if all its elements are elements of $S$. With that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- $\\{-2,0,4 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $4 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $\\{0,3 \\}$ is a subset of $S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- $\\{-3,1,3 \\}$ is *not* a subset of $S$ because it contains the element $-3 \\notin S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $A = \\{a, b, c \\}$." :question/worked-solution "To find every subset of $A$, we list out every combination of elements within $A$.\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/50/Source/Images/7bf948604295a59e82bc1c70608fce52.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\emptyset, \\{a\\}, \\{b\\}, \\{c\\}, \\{a, b\\}, \\{a, c\\}, \\{b, c\\}, \\{a, b, c\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ (the set of non-negative integers)?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{Z}$\n2. $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ (the set of negative integers)\n3. $\\mathbb{N}$" :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^-$ is *not* a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. For example, $-1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}^-$ but $-1 \\notin \\mathbb{Z}_0^+$.\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ is a subset of $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$. Every natural number is a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A =\\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid 2n \\geq 10 \\}$ and $B= \\{n\\in \\mathbb{Z} \\mid n+2 \\geq 5 \\}$?\n\n1. $A\\subseteq B$\n2. $B\\subseteq A$\n3. $B\\nsubseteq A$\n4. $B\\supseteq A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B$.\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $2n\\geq 10$, that is, $n\\geq 5$. So, we have\n$A= \\{5,6,7,8,9,\\ldots \\}$.\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n+2\\geq 5$, that is, $n\\geq 3$. So, we have\n$B= \\{3,4,5,6,7,\\ldots\\}$.\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\\subseteq B$ is true. Every element of $A$ is an element of the set $B$.\n- $B\\subseteq A$ is false, while $B\\nsubseteq A$ is true. For example, $3 \\in B$ but $3 \\notin A$.\n- $B\\supseteq A$ is equivalent to $A \\subseteq B$, which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e171efca-824b-5549-89b1-02ffcf234b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}{1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051300} {:answer/id #uuid "0bac181b-09de-540e-8569-c6dccfea9c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051301} {:answer/id #uuid "19387d58-09aa-5c2d-b6af-70038f698a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051302} {:answer/id #uuid "1a841690-0777-588c-abf9-2bd5037a07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051303} {:answer/id #uuid "397e24bc-932d-53d0-a474-6dedf9690f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19387d58-09aa-5c2d-b6af-70038f698a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051302} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0bd25e-209d-5419-930d-dfa7a7f63f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051299}] :question/id #uuid "5a3915aa-c1aa-5f3a-a352-0a780d0440c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1192" :question/problem "Find all of the subsets of $T = {1, 2, 3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc24b2d-aefe-5550-8320-e73223adc725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051366} {:answer/id #uuid "b21fb707-a876-58e3-8917-4477dbbc31bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051367} {:answer/id #uuid "ece9a89e-b697-565f-9db7-d7d4ea7f957d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051368} {:answer/id #uuid "3d705157-90e1-541c-bdbc-dbf6b491c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051369} {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429e9c42-0358-5a78-bf17-15026921143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051370} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d45657-6892-5416-b2b9-12620528d63a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051365}] :question/id #uuid "0906e8f1-346b-507f-9289-d9fb6006311a" :question/math-academy-id "q-1210" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A = {x ∈ Z: - 2 \\le x < 6}$ and $B = {x ∈ N: x + 1 < 6}$?\n\n1. $∅⊆B$\n2. $B⊆A$\n3. $A⊈B$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055155 :knowledge-point/_question